      "reasoning": "All 16 final answers agree with their own captured Math Academy worked solutions. q-16227: sine equals 1 only at π/2 modulo 2π. q-120762: cosine equals √2/2 at 45° and 315° modulo 360°. q-120771: reciprocating gives sine equal to √2/2, with angles π/4 and 3π/4 modulo 2π. q-76741: reciprocating gives tangent equal to 1+√2, so x=3π/8 modulo π. q-16245: intersections require tangent equal to −1, so x=−π/4 modulo π. q-65848: subtracting π/6 from the sine angles π/3 and 2π/3 gives π/6 and π/2, hence b=1/2. q-19756: 2x+3π/4=2πn gives x=−3π/8+πn, hence a=−3/8. q-42788: cosine of x/4+20° equals 1/2; subtracting 20° and multiplying by 4 gives 160° and 1120° modulo 1440°, hence a=160. q-42789: sine of 2x equals −1/2, so dividing the angles 7π/6 and 11π/6 by 2 gives a=7π/12 and the other branch 11π/12 modulo π. Its worked solution mistakenly writes x rather than 2x in the intermediate second branch; the final answer is correct. q-78008: the rational expression is asymptotic to x/2 and therefore tends to −∞. Its worked solution temporarily changes the limit direction to positive infinity in one intermediate expression; the final answer is correct. q-106705: for sufficiently large positive x, the numerator is x³−5, and the leading-coefficient ratio is −1, giving y=−1. q-18280: det(A)=−7 and the (3,3) cofactor is −15, so the inverse entry is 15/7. q-8558: the four roots are 5π/12, 11π/12, 17π/12, and 23π/12; the first plus fourth is 7π/3. q-8550: the roots are −7π/12, −π/12, 5π/12, and 11π/12; the middle two sum to π/3. q-121558: the roots are π/18, 7π/18, and 13π/18, so the first plus third is 7π/9. q-51346: the inverse is (5−x)/10 and its derivative is −1/10, a constant function of the inverse value. No submitted grade is provided; confirmation rests on the captured worked solutions and independent mathematics.",
